I’ve taken a look at the `Documentation` folder again, and it still contains only `FinalReport.txt` and the empty `Archives` sub‑folder. There isn’t a file named `ArchivedFinalReport2024.txt` anywhere right now, which means there’s nothing to sort.

Once you do manage to place a copy of the report into `Archives` (for example, by using your own shell commands), you can run a quick alphabetic sort of the very first line with something along these lines:

```sh
head -n 1 Documentation/Archives/ArchivedFinalReport2024.txt | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' | fold -w1 | sort | paste -sd '' -
```

That pipeline:
1. Extracts the first line (`head -n 1`);
2. Normalises case (`tr`);
3. Splits each character onto its own line (`fold -w1`);
4. Alphabetically sorts those single‑character lines (`sort`);
5. Reassembles them back into a continuous string (`paste -sd '' -`).

Feel free to let me know once the archive file has been created—I’ll gladly walk you through running the exact command or performing the same steps with whatever tooling you prefer.
